    "footer": "Name the Library · Problem-Solving Teaching · Self-contained, no logins, no data collected. · <a href=\"../../policy.html\">Privacy &amp; compliance</a>",
    "disc": "Aligned to (not reproduced from) 19 TAC Ch.113 §113.14; effect sizes from Visible Learning MetaX. This scenario is a teaching fiction.",
    "ix.0": "Grade 3 · Communities · §113.14 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "Name the Library — Who Should We Honor?",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into a town that is opening a new public library, take on a stakeholder’s point of view, and solve a real, messy question — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> Our town is opening a brand-new public library, and the community gets to choose whose name it will carry. Many people have made a difference — a scientist, a local hero, a beloved author, a civil-rights leader — but the library can only have one name. Whom should our community honor, and why?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Honor words, a jigsaw on people who make a difference, and reading a short biography as a source. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect &amp; organize",
    "ix.9": "A contributions comparison chart, primary vs. secondary sources, and a first claim. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a role, investigate, nominate a person, vote, and debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don’t open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea &amp; objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> Communities honor the people whose contributions shaped them. Choosing means learning what different people did and weighing their impact <b>fairly</b>, using evidence — that is the heart of good citizenship.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> how individuals, events, and ideas changed communities; who counts as a hero, a writer, an artist, a scientist, and a good citizen.",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same choice looks different from each stakeholder’s point of view, and a fair choice rests on evidence, not just on who we like best.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving process end to end — name a problem, gather information, weigh options, choose and implement a solution, and check it (§113.14(c)(16)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.14)",
    "ix.19": "TEKS SE",
    "ix.20": "Where it lives in the unit",
    "ix.21": "How individuals, events &amp; ideas changed communities; people who helped shape communities — Surface &amp; Deep",
    "ix.22": "Good citizenship, civic responsibility, voting — Transfer vote",
    "ix.23": "Heroic deeds of state &amp; national heroes and contemporary heroes — Surface jigsaw &amp; Transfer",
    "ix.24": "Contributions of writers &amp; artists to a community’s cultural heritage — Surface &amp; Transfer",
    "ix.25": "Individuals who created scientific breakthroughs &amp; new technology — Surface &amp; Transfer",
    "ix.26": "Gather &amp; compare primary and secondary sources, cause &amp; effect, claim + evidence, communicate — Deep &amp; Transfer",
    "ix.27": "<b>The problem-solving process — the entire Transfer phase</b>",
